        #"{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXnyve6uEz5r2Cii63Ga","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A lighthouse lens is a precisely shaped glass optic that gathers the light from a single flame or bulb and bends it into a useful beam. Early lenses were built as stacked prisms, with the rings of glass arranged so that light from the source passes through them and leaves in a nearly parallel direction. The central part of the lens, called the dioptric section, refracts light through the glass, while the outer prisms, called catadioptric rings, combine refraction and reflection to send light from above and below the lamp toward the horizon.\n\nBecause the lens concentrates light into a horizontal band, a lighthouse can be seen from far out at sea. Rotating the lens around the lamp sweeps that beam around the horizon, and the timing of each flash lets sailors identify which lighthouse they are seeing. Modern lighthouses often use sealed beacons with LED sources, but the same principle of bending light with carefully shaped glass still applies."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9747,"cache_read_input_tokens":11382,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9747},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5b0c7e2d-9a41-4f6e-8c3b-1d2e3f4a5b6c","uuid":"332fb16a-de10-4728-9fc2-b5cbd561ca77","timestamp":"2026-10-30T04:30:44.106Z","request_id":"req_011CgXnyv4PJMdsUJt44uFwb"}"#,
